1.10.4.m:
"A rat I make thy victim, O Rudra;
this is thy portion, O Rudra;
enjoy it together with thy sister Ambikā, svāhā!
We have appeased Rudra, O mother, appeased the three-eyed god,
so that he may make us more prosperous, so that he may make us better,
so that he may make us more abundant, so that he may promote us further, so that he may cause us to decide.
Healing for cow, for horse, for man healing,
also healing for us, that there may be good healing, good passage for ram and ewe!
We worship the three-eyed god, fragrant, increaser of nourishment;
as a cucumber from its stem, from death may I be released, not from immortality!
O Rudra, this is thy portion;
with it as provision go beyond the Mūjavants!
With pināka in hand, clad in a skin, with unstrung bow! //"

1.10.6.m:
Sour milk and boiled milk are a couple.
Now, what is mixed, appearing like an egg, like whey, that is an embryo.
Creatures were propagated from heat;
in that it attains boiledness, it attains heat;
therefrom he is propagated.
Now, this curd-mixture is for the All-gods.
Prajāpati created creatures;
he created them through the Vaiśvadeva itself;
therefore these creatures belong to the All-gods.
He sacrifices to the All-gods;
the All-gods are drops;
those indeed he sacrifices to.
Also, from the giving from yonder, men live upon the sacrifice.
This is the womb of creatures.
The Maruts desired it;
thence they were created seized by distress.
In that it is for the Svātavats,
it is for selfhood, for atonement.
This is the procreator of cattle.
In that the [oblation] to the Maruts is on seven potsherds,
seven are the Maruts;
the Maruts are the clan;
also, the [oblation] to the Maruts is for atonement;
also, by this he secures village-born food.

1.10.14.m:
Having created creatures, having expiated distress, he desired: "May I slay Vṛtra."
Having united with these deities, with the Maruts as the people, with Agni as the vanguard, he swooped down.
Going up to Vṛtra, seeing Vṛtra, seized by paralysis of the thighs, not daring, he stopped.
The Maruts went after him;
they went past [him].
When they reached his vital parts, then he stirred;
they heated him thoroughly then.
Therefore they are the Sāṃtapanas.
With Agni as the vanguard Indra slew Vṛtra;
therefore this [oblation] is for having a vanguard.
Moreover, Agni is the general of the gods;
therefore this is for raising an army.
Indra could not lift the thunderbolt against Vṛtra;
he poured out this share for the Maruts;
they heated him thoroughly for heroic power;
with that heroic power he raised it.
They heated him thoroughly then;
therefore they are the Sāṃtapanas.
At midday the pot of boiled rice is to be offered;
then he heats both ends.
It should be a pot of boiled rice;
for that heats him all around.
The gods did not find Vṛtra's vital part;
the Maruts pierced him with razor-sharp wheel-rims;
they heated him thoroughly then;
therefore they are the Sāṃtapanas.

1.10.15.m:
On the morrow, being about to slay Vṛtra, they dwelt in preparation.
They said: "Whose indeed shall this be tomorrow? Or cook for whom?"
They cooked this boiled rice;
with it they noticed the cattle.
They knew: "Whichever of the two these [cattle] will approach, they shall become this [master of all]."
For them they stood ready with this [food];
them they drew near with this.
That is indeed the course of the cattle.
Having victory on the morrow, they dwelt.
They said: "Whose indeed shall this be tomorrow? Or cook for whom?"
They cooked this boiled rice.
They said: "Let us not eat what has not been offered."
They offered it to the Maruts, the Gṛhamedhas.
The Maruts, the Gṛhamedhas, are cattle;
indeed to the cattle they offered it.
They were arrayed for battle.
The Asuras sent hunger against the gods.
The gods, hearing it, cooked this boiled rice.
Finding no place among the gods, it entered the Asuras again.
Then the gods flourished,
the Asuras perished.
Therefore, he who, knowing thus, cooks this boiled rice, becomes [prosperous] himself; his rival perishes.
Even with a neighbor he may cook;
he sends hunger against his rival alone.
What of the four-month sacrifices [is this], that is their domestic sacrifice, relating to cattle;
relating to cattle is the Gṛhamedha [rite].
He does not offer the fore-offerings, nor the after-offerings;
he does not recite the kindling verses.
He offers the two butter-portions,
for sacrifice-hood.
He cuts off [a share] together for Agni;
for Agni is the finished offering;
Agni is a firm standing;
for the firm standing of the finished offering.
They invoke the iḍā;
the iḍā is cattle;
relating to cattle is the Gṛhamedha [rite];
therefore they invoke the iḍā.
He deposits the scrapings,
for continuity.

1.10.18.m:
"Desiring, we summon thee; desiring, we would kindle thee; / desiring, bring the desiring fathers to eat the oblation." / Thus he recites.
For the fathers are desiring.
He recites an anuṣṭubh;
the anuṣṭubh is an end;
the fathers are an end;
therefore he recites an anuṣṭubh.
He recites one;
for the fathers have one world.
He recites three times;
for the fathers are in the third world.
In that he recites one,
by that it belongs to the fathers.
In that [he recites] three times,
by that it belongs to the gods;
both are sacrificed to.
He chooses neither the Hotṛ nor the Ṛṣi-ancestor;
he releases them from death itself.
He offers the fore-offerings without sacrificial grass;
the sacrificial grass is offspring;
he releases offspring from death itself.
One puts on his upper garment [regularly] when about to act for men;
he wears it over the right shoulder for the gods.
Now here he should have his sacred thread over the right shoulder [prācīnāvīta],
for distinction.
Having sliced off portions from the south, stepping across toward the north, standing facing the south he offers,
for the south belongs to the fathers.
First he sacrifices to Soma;
Soma is indeed the deity of the fathers;
Soma has the fathers as deity.
In that he sacrifices to Soma with the fathers,
he thereby sacrifices to the Soma-drinking fathers.
In that [he sacrifices to] those sitting on the grass,
[it is to] the sacrificers.
In that [he sacrifices to] the Agniṣvāttas,
[it is to] the householders.
In that [he sacrifices to] Agni, the conveyer of kavyas—
Agni indeed has two bodies:
with the one conveying havyas he conveys the oblation to the gods, with the one conveying kavyas to the fathers—
[it is] for the full completion, for stability.
Two indeed are the yājyā and the anuvākyā for the gods;
with the one he holds the breath, with the other he causes it to go.
Now here three are to be made;
for the fathers are further beyond than the gods;
so by that which is the third, he has indeed given beyond [them].
He cuts off portions five times;
the sacrifice is fivefold;
as great as the sacrifice is, he takes hold of it.
He pronounces the vaṣaṭ with: "Svadhā! Obeisance!"
Svadhā-call is for the fathers, obeisance for the gods;
both are sacrificed to.
He places [them] at the corners;
men indeed drink after the divine fathers;
he has sacrificed to the divine fathers.
He places [them] three times;
for we are turned toward three persons here;
he names three beyond [them],
for three are beyond: father, son, grandson,
for unbroken continuation.
He places [them] in all the corners;
for the fathers are in all directions;
he causes him to reach all directions.
He does not place [it] in that [direction];
if he should place it in that [direction], he would surround them with death;
they follow after that [direction].
Now in that he wipes [his hand] on it,
he thereby pleases it.

1.10.20.m:
Now the year became desired for him, the seasons [became] desired.
Then the Rudras were not desired for him.
In that there are these Tryambaka [offerings],
by that the Rudras become desired and pleased for him.
They are [cakes] on one potsherd;
a person is not to be obtained by [multiple] potsherds;
he reaches him singly.
Moreover, this [earth] is one;
he firmly establishes himself on this [earth] itself.
They deliberate: "Is [the offering] to be anointed with butter, or not to be anointed with butter?"
If he should anoint it with butter, he would deliver his cattle to Rudra.
That is not to be heeded;
it is to be anointed with butter indeed,
for there is no oblation that is not anointed with butter.
They carry a single firebrand;
for a single firebrand belongs to the Rudras.
They carry it smoking;
for what is smoking belongs to the Rudras.
They carry it in that direction [the north-east];
for that is the direction of the Rudras.
They carry it backwards;
they carry Rudra away backwards indeed.
Saying, "I make the mole thy beast, O Rudra," he scatters one [cake] on a mole-heap;
by that he buys off [his] cattle [from Rudra];
therefore the Lord of Cattle is destructive to them.
He should sacrifice at a crossroad;
for at a crossroad are the dwellings of the Rudras;
he buys Rudra off in his own dwellings.
"This is thy portion, O Rudra; enjoy it together with thy sister Ambikā, svāhā!"
Autumn indeed is the womb of Rudra, [namely] his sister Ambikā;
he indeed approaches [creatures] in pursuit of her;
therefore he slays most in autumn.
Together with her he buys him off.
He offers with a middle [palāśa] leaf;
for that is least harmed by the Rakṣases.
He offers with [a leaf] from the wilderness;
he buys Rudra off in the wilderness itself.
If he were to offer with a vessel, he would bring Rudra down upon creatures;
therefore he offers with [a leaf] from the wilderness.
"We have paid homage to Ambā [and] to Rudra,"
[saying which] they have become free from debt.
"Medicine for cow, for horse, medicine for man,"
[saying which] having become free from debt, they made medicine.
Saying, "We sacrifice to Tryambaka," they walk around.
There the woman desiring a husband also walks around;
that is finding a husband for her.
Then, tossing those [cakes] upward, they catch them;
they catch good fortune itself.
They pour them together for the sacrificer;
they pour good fortune together for him.
If there is one who desires a husband, they should pour [them] together for her;
they pour good fortune together for her.
Having placed them in a net-bag, he hangs them on a tree.
"O Rudra, this is thy portion; / with it as provisions, go beyond Mūjavant, / with pināka in hand, clad in a skin, with unstrung bow!"
The mountain indeed is the womb of Rudra;
from there indeed, coming down, he harms creatures;
by his own portion he causes him to go to his own womb.
They return without looking back,
so that Rudra will not follow.
They cleanse themselves outside the cowpen;
outside the cowpen indeed they buy Rudra off.
Ambī is a woman named Bhaga;
therefore they are the Tryambakas.
He whose oblation is unestablished, he is unestablished;
his Tryambaka [cakes] are unestablished.
Having returned, he should offer a caru to Aditi in ghee in the house.
Aditi is this [earth];
this is the firm foundation;
in that it is for Aditi,
he firmly establishes himself on this [earth] itself.

2.1.1.m:
One whose kinsmen have dispersed should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra and Agni.
Indra and Agni are indeed strength and virility;
with strength and virility he brings them back near.
One who has a rival should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra and Agni.
Indra and Agni are indeed strength and virility;
with strength and virility he overcomes them.
One desiring offspring, who though capable of offspring does not obtain offspring, should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra and Agni.
Indra and Agni hid Prajāpati's offspring.
He ran to them with this share;
they two gave the offspring back to him.
Indeed Indra and Agni hide the offspring of him who, though capable of offspring, does not obtain offspring.
He has approached them with a share;
they two give offspring back to him.
The offering- and invitatory verses contain [the word] "find" [or "obtain"],
for obtaining [offspring].
Setting out for battle, he should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra and Agni.
Indra and Agni are indeed strength and virility;
with strength and virility he advances against it.
Having joined battle, he should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra and Agni.
Indra and Agni are indeed strength and virility;
with strength and virility he conquers it.
When he has won the battle, he should then offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra and Agni.
He who wins a battle is deprived of strength and virility.
Indra and Agni are strength and virility;
with strength and virility they two replenish him.
Setting out toward people, he should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra and Agni, and a caru to Pūṣan.
Indra and Agni are indeed strength and virility;
with strength and virility he advances toward them.
Pūṣan is the bestower of virility;
he bestows virility upon him.
Taking possession of land, he should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra and Agni, a caru to Pūṣan, and a caru to the Lord of the Field.
Indra and Agni are indeed strength and virility;
with strength and virility they two obtain space for him.
Pūṣan is the bestower of virility;
he bestows virility upon him.
This [earth] is the mistress of the field;
in her he establishes himself.

2.1.2.m:
For a desire, he should offer [a cake] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara.
The year is Agni Vaiśvānara;
in a year desire is obtained;
he obtains the year;
he obtains the desire for which he has a desire.
Intending to plot against a neighbour, he should offer [a cake] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara.
The year is Agni Vaiśvānara;
[an oath/alliance] is contracted for a year;
having attained the year, he harms his desire [without] Varuṇa [punishing him].
Going to seek a gift, he should offer [a cake] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara.
The year is Agni Vaiśvānara;
[a gift] is received for the year;
having attained the year, he wins the gained gift.
When he has won it, he should then offer [a cake] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara.
The year is Agni Vaiśvānara;
he harnesses the year;
the year wins for him;
he makes him a sharer;
he unyokes him.
He should give the sacrificial fee to one whom he hates.
He wanders about with a noose;
he fastens it upon him.
A one-year-old bull is the sacrificial fee,
for it is the counterpart of the year.
About to eat improper food, he should offer [a cake] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara.
The year is Agni Vaiśvānara;
he conceals it for the year.
When he eats improper food, he should then offer [a cake] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara.
Whatever improper food he eats there, the year makes that palatable for him;
he eats it as palatable.
Lead or a black cloth is the sacrificial fee.
Lead is improper food; a black [cloth] is improper food;
warding off improper food with improper food, he places food-sustenance in himself.
Setting out for battle, he should offer [a cake] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara.
The year is Agni Vaiśvānara;
with the year he advances against it.
Having joined battle, he should offer [a cake] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara.
Whichever of two combatants has the firmer footing, he conquers.
This [earth] is Agni Vaiśvānara;
he makes this his footing;
on this he strides boldly forward;
he wins the battle.
When he has won the battle, he should then offer [a cake] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara.
The year is Agni Vaiśvānara;
he harnesses the year;
the year conquers for him;
he makes him a sharer;
he unyokes him.
He who desires: "May I through this king deposit these barleys or rices," should offer [a cake] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara.
The year is Agni Vaiśvānara;
the year is the giver of food-sustenance;
he has approached him with a share;
he grants him food-sustenance.
He should offer [a cake] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara and a barley caru to Varuṇa;
he should sacrifice for one who is diseased.
Seized by Varuṇa is he who is diseased;
from Varuṇa he frees him by that.
Yonder sun is Agni Vaiśvānara.

Varuṇa seizes him who is held fast by yonder [sun];
having freed him from that, as great as his self is, so much he frees from Varuṇa.

He should offer a barley caru to Varuṇa of this size, and [a cake] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara;
he should sacrifice for one desiring prosperity.
Seized by Varuṇa is he who desires prosperity;
from Varuṇa he frees him by that.
The caru is of this size;
so great is the self;
as great as his self is, having freed him from Varuṇa,
yonder sun is Agni Vaiśvānara;
he makes him take hold of yonder one;
he makes him attain the measure of yonder sun.

2.1.3.m:
He should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni Jātavedas, [one] on eleven potsherds to Dadhikrāvan, [and one] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara, who, having given all his possessions, undertakes the first offering.
Agni indeed knows of this [man] where his sacrifice is, where his good deed is;
Agni indeed finds that for him.
Impure indeed is he who gives everything;
so Dadhikrāvan makes him pure.
The year is Agni Vaiśvānara;
the year indeed knows of this [man] where his sacrifice is, where his good deed is;
the year indeed finds that for him.
He should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni Surabhimat;
he should sacrifice for one who is falsely accused.
Rathaprota Dārbhya was falsely accused;
to him the two Kaulakāvatīs said:
‘We two will sacrifice for you in such a way that they will eat your food.
Go where you do not hear of domestic cattle;
whoever approaches you, sit quite silent.’
So [they said].
Tigers, having smelled him, went away quite silently.
On the next day, having followed with the sacrificial implements, having churned fire there, the two offered [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni Surabhimat.
Then they did not bypass him.
For him whom they falsely accuse, he should sacrifice with this.
Foul-smelling, indeed, do they falsely accuse him of an offense;
this is the healing form of Agni, namely Surabhi [the fragrant];
he has provided a remedy for him;
he has made him fragrant;
he pacifies [it].
He should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni Pavamāna, [one] on eleven potsherds to Dadhikrāvan, [and one] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara, having returned home.
Pavamāna purifies him, Agni burns out [the taint].
Unpurified indeed is he whom they falsely accuse;
indeed they say: ‘Not even Dadhikrāvan could purify him’;
so Dadhikrāvan purifies him.
The year is Agni Vaiśvānara;
the year indeed sweetens him.
He should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni, [one] on eleven potsherds to Agni and Soma, [and one] on two potsherds to Heaven and Earth, who wishes to win a battle or wishes to overpower a rival.
The [cake] to Agni is for prosperity.
By Agni and Soma as heroic power, Indra slew Vṛtra;
he indeed slays Vṛtra who wins a battle or overpowers a rival;
therefore this is Vṛtra-slaying.
Indra raised the thunderbolt against Vṛtra;
Heaven and Earth did not assent to him;
they assented to him by this share.
As for the one to Heaven and Earth,
[it is] for the assent of the thunderbolt,
and so that he may have an assented thunderbolt.
When he wins a battle or overpowers a rival, then he should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni, [one] on eleven potsherds to Indra and Agni, [and one] on two potsherds to Heaven and Earth.
The [cake] to Agni is for prosperity.
By Agni and Soma as heroic power, Indra slew Vṛtra;
he was deprived of strength, of heroic power;
he saw this [cake] to Indra and Agni;
by it he placed strength and heroic power in himself.
He indeed is deprived of strength, of heroic power, who wins a battle or overpowers a rival;
Indra and Agni are strength and heroic power;
with strength, with heroic power, they two cause him to prosper.
Now, as for the one to Heaven and Earth:
to the two who assented to the thunderbolt for him, this share is made.

A Brahmin should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Agni-Soma for a desire.
A Brahmin belongs indeed to Agni-Soma by deity;
he approaches his own deity with a share for his desire;
they accomplish his desire for him, whatever his desire is.
He should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Agni-Soma;
he should sacrifice for a Brahmin desiring prosperity.
The gods indeed sat at a sacrificial session in Kurukṣetra: Agni, Soma, Indra.
They said:
'Whichever of us fame shall reach first, he shall overcome with us.'
Fame reached Soma among them.
They went together toward him;
therefore people go together toward Soma,
for he is the most famous.
Soma indeed desired that [place down below];
he went to the mountain;
Agni went after him;
the two, Agni and Soma, became united on the mountain.
Therefore Agni continuously burns on the mountain, and Soma is on the mountain.
Indra indeed considered himself as if slackened;
he went after Agni and Soma;
he said to the two:
'Sacrifice for me!'
Agni and Soma indeed sacrificed for him with this [offering].
In him Agni placed fiery brilliance, Soma virility;
then Indra became [mighty].
He who should desire prosperity, for him he should sacrifice with this;
Agni places fiery brilliance in him, Soma virility;
he indeed becomes [prosperous].
One desiring spiritual luster should offer [a cake] of millet on eleven potsherds to Agni-Soma in spring.
Agni and Soma are indeed the givers of spiritual luster;
he approaches them with their share;
they grant him spiritual luster.
He should sacrifice in spring;
spring is indeed the season of the Brahmin;
in his own season they grant him spiritual luster.
In that it is a cake, thereby it belongs to Agni;
in that it is of millet, thereby it belongs to Soma;
he has delivered the entire sacrificial offering to Agni-Soma;
they grant him all spiritual luster.
The two samyājyā verses should be addressed to Soma and Agni.
Agni is fiery brilliance;
Soma is virility;
with fiery brilliance and with virility he grasps spiritual luster on both sides for him.
One desiring cattle should offer a caru of half-ground [grain] to Soma-Pūṣan.
Soma is the depositor of semen;
Pūṣan is the progenitor of cattle;
Soma deposits semen for him;
Pūṣan brings forth cattle.
One desiring the office of purohita should offer a caru to Soma-Indra.
A Brahmin belongs to Soma by deity;
a nobleman belongs to Indra;
Soma indeed grants what cannot be taken away;
he approaches him with his share;
he grants him a realm that cannot be taken away.
One who has harmed a fellow, or one who is diseased, should offer a caru to Agni-Varuṇa.
He who harms a fellow indeed commits an untruth;
he who commits an untruth offends against the deity.
Agni is all deities;
here indeed is that deity also against whom he offended; from that he frees him.
In that it belongs to Varuṇa,
from Varuṇa he frees him thereby.
This ... is performed: an expiation of everything, an atonement of everything.

2.1.5.m:
One desiring spiritual luster should offer a caru of white rice in ghee to Soma-Rudra.
Svarbhānu the Asura pierced the sun with darkness;
Soma and Rudra healed him;
they drove away his blemish with this very [offering];
with this they placed fiery brilliance in him.
He who should desire spiritual luster, for him he should sacrifice with this;
he drives away his blemish;
he places fiery brilliance in him.
The caru is of this size;
so great is the self;
as great as his self is, so much fiery brilliance he places in him.
The rice grains are white; they milk white cows for ghee;
fiery brilliance is collected thereby.
Ghee is the sprinkling;
they wipe themselves with ghee;
it is in ghee;
he places further fiery brilliance in him.
On whatever night he is about to sacrifice in the morning, they should not carry water into his house for that night;
water is pacification;
they would appease [the fire].
They sacrifice in an enclosed space,
for the securing of fiery brilliance.
They proceed along with the rays [of the sun];
along with the rays they strike away his blemish.
He should sacrifice at the full moon with Tiṣya;
Soma is the moon;
Rudra is Tiṣya;
he has approached them directly.
The plants belonging to Soma face eastward;
those belonging to Rudra face westward;
for they do not dry up toward the east; they dry up toward the west.
The verses are of Manu;
whatever Manu spoke, that he spoke as medicine;
that is for the sake of medicine.
These are Śakvarī verses,
for power;
they are Nārāśaṃsī verses,
for peace.
From leprosy indeed is fear for him;
for they drive it far away.
One desiring cattle should offer a caru of half-ground [grain] to Soma-Pūṣan.
A Brahmin belongs to Soma by deity;
cattle are Pūṣan;
he strengthens his own deity with cattle;
he has indeed made a skin.

2.1.6.m:
Practicing sorcery, he should offer a caru of black rice to Soma-Rudra.
The plants belong to Soma;
having begged him away from that, he shuts up his cattle for Rudra.
The rice grains are black;
black is darkness;
darkness is death;
he causes him to be seized by death alone.
The barhis is made of śara-reed; the firewood is of vibhīdaka wood.
Thinking, 'May he crush [him]',
the barhis is of śara-reed; for division the firewood is of vibhīdaka wood.
He who should desire, 'May I produce a second one in his realm', should offer a caru of unselected rice in butter-milk to Soma-Rudra.
These creatures belong to Soma;
he produces a second one in his realm.
Half the barhis is of śara-reed, half not of śara-reed;
half the firewood is of vibhīdaka wood, half not of vibhīdaka wood;
he produces a second one in his realm.
He should offer a curd-mixture to Soma-Rudra;
he should sacrifice for one who is diseased.
Dead, he belongs to Agni;
living, to Soma;
from both sides he buys him off.
Man is milk;
milk ails in him;
by milk he buys off his milk.
The Hotṛ should have his eyes bandaged;
having led him away to the wilderness, he should make him open his eyes;
he should give an ox to him;
he should slaughter it;
he should eat of it;
if he should not eat of it, he would die.

2.1.7.m:
One practicing sorcery, or one against whom sorcery is practiced, should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Agni-Viṣṇu;
he should also sacrifice with ghee for Sarasvatī.
Agni is all deities;
with the deities he counteracts his deities;
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice;
with the sacrifice, the sacrifice;
Sarasvatī is speech;
with speech, speech.
Thereby he has employed [it] after counteracting [the attack];
and further, having counteracted, he has employed [it] over [him].
One desiring sight should offer a caru in ghee to Agni-Viṣṇu.
Agni is the giver of sight of men, Viṣṇu of the gods;
these two are the givers of sight;
he approaches them with a share;
they grant him sight.
Ghee is the milk of the cow, rice grains are of the draft ox;
that is a pair;
sight is a pair;
with a pair they produce a pair of sight for him.
Ghee is milk;
sight is milk;
with milk they produce milk-like sight for him.
Ghee is fiery brilliance;
sight is fiery brilliance;
with fiery brilliance they produce fiery-brilliant sight for him.
He gives gold;
gold is lifespan;
sight is lifespan;
with lifespan he places lifespan and sight in him.
It is of a hundred measures;
man has a lifespan of a hundred, a strength of a hundred;
he obtains lifespan and strength.
One against whom sorcery is practiced should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Agni-Viṣṇu.
Agni is all deities;
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice;
he enters between the deities and the sacrifice in order to overcome [the rival].
In battle he should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Agni-Viṣṇu;
he should also sacrifice with ghee for Sarasvatī.
Agni is all deities;
with the deities he repels his deities;
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice;
with the sacrifice, the sacrifice;
Sarasvatī is speech;
with speech, speech.
If he should think, 'They are attacking from the front', he should make two invitatory verses and one offering verse.
With two [the balance] is made equal; with one he prevails.
He whose rival should sacrifice with soma should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni-Viṣṇu in the morning, a caru to Sarasvatī, a caru to Bṛhaspati; [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Agni-Viṣṇu at midday, a caru to Sarasvatī, a caru to Bṛhaspati; [a cake] on twelve potsherds to Agni-Viṣṇu in the afternoon, a caru to Sarasvatī, a caru to Bṛhaspati.
Agni is all deities;
with the deities he obtains his deities;
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice;
with the sacrifice, the sacrifice;
Sarasvatī is speech;
with speech, speech;
Bṛhaspati is the sacred word;
with the sacred word he obtains his sacred word;
with the potsherds, the meters;
with the cakes, the soma-pressings.
He should offer [a cake] on one potsherd to Mitra-Varuṇa, or a curd offering;
with this he obtains the anūbandhyā cow.
This is the sacrifice-modeling offering;
with it he obtains the sacrifice.

2.1.8.m:
One desiring rain should offer a caru to Agni-Maruts.
They should make the caru and the pot from the same clay.
In the very fire where they cook the caru, they should fumigate the pot.
Smoke goes from here to yonder, not the flame;
therefore they fumigate it, they do not cook it.
When they set down the oblations, then, having placed the pot on the southern hip [of the altar], they should fill it with water.
If it should break before the completion [of the rite], he should say: 'It will rain today.'
If at the completion, he should say: 'It will rain tomorrow.'
If it should break after a long time, he should say: 'We do not know for sure.'
Agni seeks rain from here; the Maruts send it down from yonder.
These are the givers of rain;
he approaches them with a share;
they grant him rain.
One desiring a village or desiring cattle should offer a caru of priyaṅgu-millet in milk to the Maruts.
In the milk of speckled cows, there should be ghee of speckled cows;
into that they should also sprinkle cow-urine.
When the speckled one milked, that became priyaṅgu-millet;
this [earth] or speech is the speckled one;
this priyaṅgu-millet is her milk;
with her own milk he approaches her.
The invitatory and offering verses contain the word 'dear' (priya);
he makes him dear to his kinsmen.
They are two-footed and four-footed;
he wins for him both two-footed and four-footed cattle.
Just as the calf stretches toward the udder, or the cow toward the calf, so the kinsmen stretch toward him.
He whose two twin sons or twin calves should be born should offer [a cake] on thirteen potsherds to the Maruts.
A twin person being born bodes childlessness; a cow, cattleness.
That which is thirteenth;
Prajāpati is the thirteenth;
he obtains Prajāpati.
That which is twelfth, is by means of the year;
that the invitatory verse is a gāyatrī, is by means of the year;
that the offering verse is a jagatī, is for cattle;
that there is an offering to the Maruts, is for cattle.
Where the common people should attempt to overpower the king, he should offer [a cake] on seven potsherds to the Maruts.
The Kayāśubhīya of Agastya should be the kindling verses and the invitatory and offering verses.
Agastya sprinkled oxen for the Maruts;
he slaughtered them for Indra.
They flew at him, taking the thunderbolt;
with this [hymn] he pacified them;
with this he pacifies them.
It is on seven potsherds;
for the Maruts are seven;
the Maruts are the common people;
with their own share he pacifies them.

2.1.10.m:
He whose well-known sacrifice is omitted should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni Pathikṛt.
He goes off the path whose well-known sacrifice is omitted.
Agni is the path-maker of the gods;
he approached him with his share;
he leads him back to the path.
An ox is the sacrificial fee,
for it draws along the path.
He who, being one who has established his sacred fires, should go abroad should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni Vratapati.
He violates many a vow who, being one who has established his sacred fires, goes abroad,
for on a day of vow he either approaches a woman or eats meat.
Agni is the lord of vows of the gods;
he approached him with his share;
he causes him to take hold of the vow.
He who, being one who has established his sacred fires, should shed tears should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni Vratabhṛt.
He who has established his sacred fires is brought to the gods;
therefore he must not shed tears,
for the gods do not shed tears.
Agni is the bearer of vows of the gods;
his vow went to Agni;
from him he takes hold of the further vow.
One against whom sorcery is practiced should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni Yaviṣṭha.
With whatever employments the other one employs [sorcery] against him, the youngest [Agni] keeps them away from him.
In battle he should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni Vājasṛt.
He desires to win the prize who wishes to win a battle;
Agni is the prize-runner of the gods;
he approached him with his share;
he runs for the prize for him.
In battle he should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni Anīkavant.
Whithersoever the army-face of Agni goes, that is not to be resisted;
Agni conquers the armies for him.
He should also sacrifice with ghee for Viṣṇu.
From this Viṣṇu conquered these worlds;
following the conquest of Viṣṇu, he conquers these worlds;
he thrusts the rival away from these worlds.
He who should desire, 'May I shut up his cattle for Rudra', should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni Rudravant.
Agni is Rudra;
for Rudra he shuts up his cattle.
If he should desire, 'May it be appeased', he should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni Surabhimat.
This is Agni's healing body, namely the fragrant one;
he made medicine for him;
he made him fragrant;
he indeed appeases [it].
He who should desire, 'May I be possessed of food, an eater of food, and a lord of food', should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni Annavant, Annāda, and Annapati.
Agni is possessed of food, an eater of food, and a lord of food among the gods;
he approached him with his share;
he makes him possessed of food, an eater of food, and a lord of food.

2.2.1.m:
'O Ādityas, I shall make a portion for you; bring back so-and-so, son of so-and-so,' he should say when about to offer the oblation.
He should offer this caru to the Ādityas in ghee.
These creatures belong to the Ādityas;
he has approached them;
they bring him back.
Seven pegs of aśvattha-wood should lie within the altar.
At the completion [of the rite], he should drive them through into the middle pole of the chariot-stand:
'Here I bind the Ādityas until the return of so-and-so.'
Thus he binds the Ādityas;
they, seeking release, bring him back.
The virility of the common people departed;
it entered the aśvattha-tree;
by that virility it became quivering.
Thus by this he secures the virility of the common people.
If he should not return in seven [days], having placed them in the kindling-wood, he should offer this very oblation.
These creatures belong to the Ādityas;
he has approached them;
they bring him back.
If in seven [days],
twenty-one are the sevenfold Ādityas;
he has approached them;
they bring him back.
If he should return in one [group of seven] or in two, they would be liable to expel him.
Now, since he returns in all, he returns as one who cannot be expelled.
When he has returned, then he should offer a caru in ghee to the sustaining Ādityas.
The Ādityas are the expellers;
the Ādityas are the returners;
they sustain him.
'O Aditi, assent, true wish, here the mind!' with this [formula] he should take up the footprint of the expelled king.
Having gone around with the wind to the assembly-hall of him who would be a village-spokesman in the east, they should scatter it:
'Go forth, O self-strong Maruts, with this lord of the clan to yonder king!' with this [formula] they should bring rice from his house.
He should separate it threefold.
Those [grains] which are black, he should tie them up in a black antelope skin and put them down.
Those which are white, he should offer as this caru to the Ādityas in ghee.
These creatures belong to Aditi;
he has approached them;
they bring him back.
Then those [grains] from which he separated them, having gone north, having broken up a mound of earth from an anthill, he should sacrifice:
'What in your terrible mouth today I sacrifice, for the release of these bonds, / you whom people foolishly rejoice in as earth, / I know you completely as Nirṛti.'
He who is expelled is seized by Nirṛti;
thereby he releases him from Nirṛti.
In this way he has returned to the common people;
now his kingship has not returned.
Therefore, those grains of rice which are black, he should offer them as a caru to Varuṇa in ghee.
Varuṇa is the king among the gods;
he is the returner of kingship;
he has approached him with his share;
he brings him back to kingship.
This indeed belongs to diverse clans;
by this he returns to both kinds of clans, the divine and the human.

2.2.2.m:
One who desires spiritual luster should offer a caru of white rice in ghee to Sūrya.
There should be a gold ornament of a hundred measures below;
there should be a golden ornament of a hundred measures above.
Yonder sun indeed was here;
having grasped him with these two, they placed him above these creatures.
Sitting above, yonder sun eats these creatures.
This [earth] is the silver one; that [sky] is the golden one.
Grasping him with these two, he places him above these creatures.
Sitting above, he eats these creatures.
He should also offer five kṛṣṇalas at the fore-offerings.
By these [five], yonder sun is endowed with splendor along these five seasons;
he makes him endowed with splendor along these five quarters.
One who desires long life should offer a caru of a hundred kṛṣṇalas in ghee to Prajāpati.
The gods, having slain the Asuras, feared death;
those gods ran to Prajāpati.
Prajāpati caused them to sacrifice with this [offering];
then the gods went to immortality.
Gold indeed is immortality;
long life is immortality;
with immortality having attained [it], he placed immortal life in them.
One who desires long life should cause him to sacrifice with this [offering].
This indeed is the immortality of a mortal, that he attains his full lifespan.
Gold indeed is immortality;
long life is immortality;
with immortality having attained [it], he places immortal life in him;
by that he attains his full lifespan;
he does not die before his full lifespan.
It consists of a hundred kṛṣṇalas;
man has a hundred years of life and a hundredfold virility;
he attains life and virility.
He cuts off four kṛṣṇalas each time
for success.
He says that everything is to be carried around to the Brahman priest;
he establishes the whole sacrifice in the Brahman priest;
the Brahman priest places all virility in him.
Moreover, the Brahman priest is the Brahman;
through the Brahman the Brahman priest places long life in him.

2.2.3.m:
One who desires the office of purohita should offer a caru to Bṛhaspati.
Its yājyā and anuvākyā to Bṛhaspati should be luminous.
Bṛhaspati is indeed the Brahman;
the Brahmin is of Bṛhaspati by deity.
He has approached his own deity for the office of purohita;
his own deity causes him to attain the office of purohita.
That which wavers and shines out, that is light;
therefore [the verses] are luminous.
If he should not quite attain the office of purohita, he should offer an oblation to Indra and Bṛhaspati.
He has made the Brahman and the Kṣatra yoked together;
they immediately appoint him as purohita.
One who desires a village or desires cattle should offer a caru to Bṛhaspati in milk.
Its yājyā and anuvākyā to Bṛhaspati should contain [the word] 'group' (gaṇa).
They should bring milk from the house of him who is prosperous in many men.
It should be the milk of their own cows;
it should be water;
it is prosperity that is thus gathered.
Bṛhaspati is indeed the Brahman;
Bṛhaspati is the meters;
through the meters Bṛhaspati is accompanied by a group;
he thus presents his own deity with the most [abundant];
he makes him accompanied by a group of kinsmen.
He should offer a caru to Brahmaṇaspati in battle.
Its yājyā and anuvākyā to Brahmaṇaspati should contain [the words] 'hero' (vīra) and 'vital power' (vayas).
He should cause him to sacrifice where the roof [of the enemy's dwelling] is seen.
The prastara should have its barhis pointing upward;
the enclosing-sticks should have arrows;
birds enter the other village—thus it is to be understood: 'We shall conquer.'
Brahmaṇaspati is indeed the Brahman;
he has approached the Brahman itself.
He should offer a caru to Brahmaṇaspati where he desires: 'May there be the power of the Brahman.'
Its yājyā and anuvākyā to Brahmaṇaspati should be accompanied by the Maruts.
Brahmaṇaspati is indeed the Brahman;
the common people are the Maruts;
he thus establishes the common people upon the Brahman,
for firmness, for non-slackness.

2.2.4.m:
One who desires cattle should offer a caru of garmut-grass to Bṛhaspati.
Prajāpati created cattle;
created from him, they went away.
Bṛhaspati followed them;
where they settled, there garmut-grass arose from the dung.
He said to him:
'Cause me to sacrifice with this.'
He caused him to sacrifice with it;
cattle turned toward him.
One who desires cattle should cause him to sacrifice with this [offering];
cattle indeed turn toward him;
for when he caused him to sacrifice with this, then cattle turned toward him.
One who desires cattle should offer a caru of garmut-grass to Soma and Pūṣan.
Prajāpati created cattle;
created from him, they went away.
He made Pūṣan their overlord;
where they settled, there garmut-grass arose from the dung.
He brought it:
'By this [is] my support.'
Soma said:
'What ripens without tilling is mine.'
He offered it to Soma and Pūṣan;
cattle turned toward him.
One who desires cattle should cause him to sacrifice with this [offering];
cattle indeed turn toward him;
for when he caused him to sacrifice with this, then cattle turned toward him.
Soma is indeed the placer of semen;
Pūṣan is the procreator of cattle;
Soma places semen in him;
Pūṣan procreates cattle.
One who desires cattle should offer a caru of garmut-grass to Prajāpati.
It should be in the milk of speckled cows; the ghee should be of speckled cows.
They should also sprinkle cow's urine into it.
When they milked the speckled [cow], that became garmut-grass.
This [earth] or speech is the speckled one;
its head is the garmut-grass;
therefore it is like an egg, like biestings.
One who desires cattle should offer that caru to Prajāpati.
Cattle belong to Prajāpati;
Prajāpati is the procreator of cattle;
he has approached him with his own share;
he procreates cattle for him.
He should offer a caru made of vāstva-grain to Rudra where Rudra afflicts creatures.
Vāstva is born of the site (vāstu);
what is made of vāstva belongs indeed to Rudra;
with his own share he appeases him.
He should cause a Niṣāda chieftain to sacrifice with it;
for that is his iṣṭi;
a hornless or a lop-eared donkey is the dakṣiṇā.

2.2.5.m:
One who desires, 'May I be the highest (parameṣṭhin),' should offer [a cake] on twelve potsherds to Parameṣṭhin.
Parameṣṭhin indeed is he among the gods who is highest;
the Rājanya is the highest among men.
He approaches him with his own share;
he makes him the highest.
He should hand him a bow and three [arrows, saying]: 'May Agni bestow splendor upon you; may Indra [bestow] indriyá-power, ancestral kinship.'
Agni indeed bestows splendor upon him;
Indra [bestows] indriyá-power, ancestral kinship.
One who has a rival should offer [a cake] on twelve potsherds to the All-gods.
Having placed it upon the barhis, he should divide it in the middle with the wooden sword, [saying]: 'Here do I divide myself and such-and-such,' [naming] the one whom he hates.
What is crushed down and what adheres to the wooden sword, he should cut off for Viṣṇu of Wide Strides.
Viṣṇu indeed strides between them.
He should sweep it back together, [saying]: 'Here do I sweep together myself and such-and-such,' [naming] the one who is dear to him.
These creatures are indeed belonging to the All-gods;
with them he divides off the other;
with them he unites himself.
When an army is setting out, he should offer a caru to Indrāṇī.
Indrāṇī indeed is the army;
by means of the Brahman he sharpens it from the front, from the mouth, for victory.
There should also be balbaja-grass in the kindling-wood;
these [blades of grass] have indeed arisen from dung;
he leads it forth in the proper manner.
Whatever he may obtain in that army, that is the sacrificial gift.
One who desires prosperity should offer a caru to Vācaspati.
He who is the overseer of speech, he is Vācaspati;
that is indeed a means of producing prosperity.

2.2.7.m:
One whose gold is lost, or who finds gold, should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni, a caru to Savitṛ, gruel to Vāyu—or fresh milk—[and a cake] on one potsherd to Bhūmi.
Agni indeed found gold in the beginning;
he consulted with Savitṛ;
there is indeed nothing that is unapproached by Vāyu;
upon this [earth] he found it.
The [offering] to Agni is for acquisition;
the one to Savitṛ is for impulsion;
the one to Vāyu is for bringing [it] forth.
Then, as to the one to Bhūmi:
upon her indeed he found it.
These indeed are the givers;
he approaches them with their share;
they bestow [it] upon him.
When he finds [it], then he should offer to these very ones;
he gives a share to those very ones by whom he found it,
for harmlessness.
Prajāpati gave his daughters, the lunar mansions (nakṣatras), to King Soma.
He dwelled only with Rohiṇī, not with the others.
Not being approached, they returned [to their father].
He seized him with consumption (rājayakṣma);
he wasted away.
Therefore, one seized by consumption wastes away.
He ran up to Prajāpati;
Prajāpati said to him:
'Speak the truth.'
He spoke the truth:
'So that I may dwell equally among all.'
Therefore he dwells equally among all.
Prajāpati sought an expiation for him;
he offered a caru to the All-gods for him on the new-moon day;
thereby he found an expiation for him.
He swelled along with that one [the moon] swelling;
he became better off.
For one who is seized by consumption, he should offer a caru to the All-gods on the new-moon day.
By this [offering] he indeed found an expiation for him;
by it he finds an expiation for him.
He swells along with that one swelling;
he becomes better off.

2.2.8.m:
Prajāpati distributed the shares to the gods.
Indra said:
'Whatever is left over, that is mine.'
What was left over was indriyá-power.
It rose upward along these worlds.
He did not reach it with one [cake];
nor with two;
having reached it with the third, he obtained it.
In that there are three cakes,
having reached the indriyá-power and virility from these worlds, he obtains [them].
Each succeeding cake is larger;
for each succeeding world is greater.
The first is for Indra the King;
the middle one is for Indra the Self-ruler;
the last is for Indra the Overlord.
Indra became all these: kingship, self-rule, overlordship.
He who sacrifices with these becomes all these;
he who knows thus becomes all this.
Having recited the invitatory verse of the first, he should sacrifice with the offering verse of the middle one;
having recited that of the middle one, he should sacrifice with that of the last;
having recited that of the last, he should sacrifice with that of the first.
In this way, all [verses] become invitatory verses for him, all become offering verses,
for the recognition of these worlds,
and also for continuous connection.
One desiring spiritual luster should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra accompanied by the Gharma-vessel, accompanied by the Sun.
The Gharma-vessel is splendor;
the Sun is splendor;
he obtains splendor itself.
One desiring cattle should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra accompanied by indriyá-power.
Cattle are indriyá-power;
Indra is the giver of indriyá-power;
he approaches him with his share;
he bestows indriyá-power and cattle upon him.

2.2.9.m:
One should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra accompanied by the Arka, accompanied by the horse sacrifice (Aśvamedha);
he should make an expelled [chieftain] sacrifice.
He who is expelled has indeed reached the end;
the Arka is the end;
the horse sacrifice is the end;
by means of the end, he provides for him at the end.
A barren cow is the sacrificial fee,
[thinking:] 'May he bring [them] into subjection to me.'
One who desires, 'May I prosper among people,' should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra accompanied by the Arka, accompanied by the horse sacrifice.
People are indeed the end of prosperities;
the Arka is the end;
the horse sacrifice is the end;
by means of the end, he provides for him at the end.
A barren cow is the sacrificial fee,
[thinking:] 'May he bring [them] into subjection to me.'
One who has attained fortune should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra accompanied by the Arka, accompanied by the horse sacrifice.
When one reaches the end of fortune, he then becomes worse off;
the Arka is the end;
the horse sacrifice is the end;
he takes hold of the end itself;
he does not become worse off.
One who desires, 'May a great sacrifice fall to my lot,' should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra accompanied by the Arka, accompanied by the horse sacrifice.
These indeed are the two sacrificial bodies of Indra: the Arka and the horse sacrifice;
he takes hold of those two;
by means of them, he takes hold of the great sacrifice.
He should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra accompanied by the Gharma-vessel, accompanied by the Sun;
[a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra accompanied by wrath, accompanied by mind;
[a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra accompanied by indriyá-power;
[a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra accompanied by the Arka, accompanied by the horse sacrifice;
he should make one desiring prosperity sacrifice.
This [earth] of Indra is the Gharma-vessel and the Sun;
this is his wrath and mind;
this is his Indra and indriyá-power;
this is his Arka and horse sacrifice.
Indra became all these;
he who sacrifices with these becomes all these;
he who knows thus becomes all this.

2.2.10.m:
One should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra, the Deliverer from Distress (Aṃhomuc);
he should make a diseased person sacrifice.
This indeed is Indra's healing body: the Deliverer from Distress;
he approaches him with his share;
he releases him from distress.
One who fears losing his possessions, death, or expulsion should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra the Protector (Trātṛ).
Indra is the protector;
Indra is the expeller;
he approaches him with his share;
he protects him.
A senior kinsman should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra who keeps [men] upright (Anvṛju).
The senior kinsman is indriyá-power;
by means of indriyá-power, he makes them upright.
In battle, one should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra the Roaring (Prababhra).
Indra, roaring, hurled the thunderbolt at Vṛtra;
he hurls the thunderbolt roaring against them.
In battle, one should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra the Dispeller of Foes (Vaimṛdha).
He who wins a battle smites away the foes;
he smites away the foes.
In battle, one should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra the Slayer of Assailants (Abhimātihan).
He who wins a battle slays assailants;
he slays assailants.
One who desires, 'May I overpower [them]; may I have safety,' should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra the Overpowerer of Assailants (Abhimātiṣāh).
Indra raised the thunderbolt against Vṛtra;
the Dānavas did not yield to him;
they yielded to him with this share;
then he slew the assailants;
he slew Vṛtra.
He who wins a battle indeed slays Vṛtra;
therefore, this is Vṛtra-slaying.

2.2.11.m:
One should offer [a cake] on one potsherd to Indra;
he should make an expelled [chieftain] sacrifice.
'Come forward from the furthest distance'—these should be the invitiation and offering verses.
He who is expelled has indeed gone to the distance;
having reached him from the distance, he brings him back.
One should offer [a cake] on thirteen potsherds to Indra;
he should make an expelled [chieftain] sacrifice.
The thirteenth is redundant;
the expelled one is redundant;
from what is redundant, having reached him who is redundant, he brings him back.
One should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra the Thunderbolt-bearer;
[a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra the Vṛtra-slayer;
[a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra the Overcomer of Vṛtra, [for one] whose rival sacrifices with Soma.
He who sacrifices with Soma raises a thunderbolt against his rival.
In that it is for the Thunderbolt-bearer,
by means of the thunderbolt he lays low his thunderbolt.
In that it is for the Vṛtra-slayer,
his rival is indeed Vṛtra;
he slays him.
In that it is for the Overcomer of Vṛtra,
his rival is indeed Vṛtra;
he overcomes him.
One who disputes over land or cattle should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra the Conqueror of the Field (Kṣetrañjaya).
Indra is the conqueror of the field among the gods;
he approaches him with his share;
he conquers the field and cattle for him.
Where kings are nearly equal, one should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra the Overlord (Adhirāja).
Indra is the overlord among the gods;
he approaches him with his share;
he brings him to paramount rule.
